      "text": "Let \\( x \\) represent Jane's current age. According to the problem, one year ago Jane's age was a perfect square, so her age one year ago was \\( x - 1 \\). One year from now, her age will be \\( x + 1 \\), and this age is a perfect cube. Therefore, we can write the following equations:\n\n\\[\nx - 1 = n^2\n\\]\n\\[\nx + 1 = k^3\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) and \\( k \\) are integers. From the first equation, we can express \\( x \\) in terms of \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\nx = n^2 + 1\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this expression for \\( x \\) into the second equation, we get:\n\n\\[\nn^2 + 1 + 1 = k^3\n\\]\n\\[\nn^2 + 2 = k^3\n\\]\n\nWe need to find integers \\( n \\) and \\( k \\) that satisfy this equation. Let's test some values for \\( n \\):\n\n1. If \\( n = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   1^2 + 2 = 3 \\quad \\text{(not a perfect cube)}\n   \\]\n\n2. If \\( n = 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   2^2 + 2 = 6 \\quad \\text{(not a perfect cube)}\n   \\]\n\n3. If \\( n = 3 \\):\n   \\[\n   3^2 + 2 = 11 \\quad \\text{(not a perfect cube)}\n   \\]\n\n4. If \\( n = 4 \\):\n   \\[\n   4^2 + 2 = 18 \\quad \\text{(not a perfect cube)}\n   \\]\n\n5. If \\( n = 5 \\):\n   \\[\n   5^2 + 2 = 27 \\quad \\text{(27 is a perfect cube, since } 27 = 3^3)\n   \\]\n   So, \\( k = 3 \\). Substituting \\( n = 5 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\) back into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\n   \\[\n   x = 5^2 + 1 = 25 + 1 = 26\n   \\]\n\nTo verify, we check that one year ago Jane was 25 years old, which is a perfect square (\\( n = 5 \\)), and one year from now, Jane will be 27 years old, which is a perfect cube (\\( k = 3 \\)).\n\nTherefore, Jane is \\(\\boxed{26}\\) years old.",
