      "text": "Let the current age of Jane be \\( x \\) years. One year ago, her age was \\( x - 1 \\) years, and one year from now, her age will be \\( x + 1 \\) years. According to the problem, one year ago, Jane's age was a perfect square, and one year from now, her age will be a perfect cube.\n\nThis gives us the following equations:\n\\[ x - 1 = k^2 \\]\n\\[ x + 1 = m^3 \\]\nwhere \\( k \\) and \\( m \\) are integers.\n\nFrom the first equation, we can express \\( x \\) as:\n\\[ x = k^2 + 1 \\]\n\nSubstituting this expression for \\( x \\) into the second equation, we get:\n\\[ k^2 + 1 + 1 = m^3 \\]\n\\[ k^2 + 2 = m^3 \\]\n\\[ m^3 - k^2 = 2 \\]\n\nWe need to find integer solutions for \\( k \\) and \\( m \\) that satisfy this equation. Let's test some values for \\( m \\):\n\n1. If \\( m = 2 \\):\n\\[ m^3 = 8 \\]\n\\[ k^3 - k^2 = 6 \\]\nThis does not yield an integer solution for \\( k \\).\n\n2. If \\( m = 3 \\):\n\\[ m^3 = 27 \\]\n\\[ k^3 - k^2 = 15 \\]\nThis does not yield an integer solution for \\( k \\).\n\n3. If \\( m = 4 \\):\n\\[ m^3 = 64 \\]\n\\[ k^3 - k^2 = 36 \\]\nThis does not yield an integer solution for \\( k \\).\n\n4. If \\( m = 5 \\):\n\\[ m^3 = 125 \\]\n\\[ k^3 - k^2 = 57 \\]\nThis does not yield an integer solution for \\( k \\).\n\n5. If \\( m = 6 \\):\n\\[ m^3 = 216 \\]\n\\[ k^3 - k^2 = 102 \\]\nThis does not yield an integer solution for \\( k \\).\n\n6. If \\( m = 7 \\):\n\\[ m^3 = 343 \\]\n\\[ k^3 - k^2 = 194 \\]\nThis does not yield an integer solution for \\( k \\).\n\n7. If \\( m = 8 \\):\n\\[ m^3 = 512 \\]\n\\[ k^3 - k^2 = 160 \\]\n\\[ k^2(k - 1) = 160 \\]\n\\[ k(k - 1) = 160 \\]\nThe factor pairs of 160 are (1, 160), (2, 80), (4, 40), (5, 32), (8, 20), and (10, 16). The only pair that gives integer values for \\( k \\) is (10, 16), since \\( k = 11 \\).\n\nSo, \\( k = 11 \\). Substituting \\( k = 11 \\) back into the equation \\( x = k^2 + 1 \\):\n\\[ x = 11^2 + 1 = 121 + 1 = 122 \\]\n\nTherefore, Jane's current age is \\(\\boxed{122}\\).",
