      "text": "Let \\( n \\) be Jane's current age. One year ago, her age was \\( n - 1 \\), and this number is a perfect square, so we can write:\n\\[\nn - 1 = k^2\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nn = k^2 + 1.\n\\]\n\nOne year from now, Jane's age will be \\( n + 1 \\), and this number is a perfect cube. So, we can write:\n\\[\nn + 1 = m^3\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Substituting \\( n = k^2 + 1 \\) into this equation, we get:\n\\[\nk^2 + 1 + 1 = m^3 \\implies k^2 + 2 = m^3.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find integer solutions to the equation \\( k^2 + 2 = m^3 \\). We will test small values of \\( k \\) to see if the equation holds.\n\n1. If \\( k = 1 \\):\n\\[\n1^2 + 2 = 1 + 2 = 3 \\quad \\text{(not a cube)}.\n\\]\n\n2. If \\( k = 2 \\):\n\\[\n2^2 + 2 = 4 + 2 = 6 \\quad \\text{(not a cube)}.\n\\]\n\n3. If \\( k = 3 \\):\n\\[\n3^2 + 2 = 9 + 2 = 11 \\quad \\text{(not a cube)}.\n\\]\n\n4. If \\( k = 4 \\):\n\\[\n4^2 + 2 = 16 + 2 = 18 \\quad \\text{(not a cube)}.\n\\]\n\n5. If \\( k = 5 \\):\n\\[\n5^2 + 2 = 25 + 2 = 27 \\quad \\text{(which is } 3^3 \\text{)}.\n\\]\nSo, \\( k = 5 \\) is a solution. Therefore, Jane's current age is:\n\\[\nn = k^2 + 1 = 5^2 + 1 = 25 + 1 = 26.\n\\]\n\nTo verify, if Jane is 26 years old one year ago, she was \\( 26 - 1 = 25 \\), which is a perfect square (\\( 5^2 \\)). And one year from now, she will be \\( 26 + 1 = 27 \\), which is a perfect cube (\\( 3^3 \\)).\n\nThus, Jane is \\(\\boxed{26}\\) years old.",
